# From the StatClaim article, "Simulation and its uncertainty". Power of a
# pooled two-sample t-test, by formula and by 10,000 simulated studies.
#
# Both checks report that the 80% power target is not met. That is the expected
# answer, not an error: with 40 per arm, a difference of 5 with a standard
# deviation of 10 gives about 61% power. The example compares two ways of
# finding power; it is not a design that is big enough. About 64 per arm gives
# 80%.
use models.two_arm_continuous
use sampling
use tests.t
use planning

# 40 participants per arm, normal outcomes.
let study = two_arm_continuous(n_control=40, n_treatment=40)
let analysis = t_test(design=fixed(model=study),
                      null=equal_means(model=study), alpha=1/20)
# The target: 80% power when the true difference is 5 and the SD is 10, half a
# standard deviation.
let target = power_target(at=mean_difference(value=5, sd=10),
                          minimum=4/5, maximum_typeI=1/20)
# By a normal formula: power about 0.609, so "target not met". This is
# expected; see the description above.
let formula = calculate(meets(analysis=analysis, target=target))
# By simulation: power about 0.603 (95% interval 0.593 to 0.612), close to the
# formula. The whole interval is below 80%, so the target is not met; one that
# straddled 80% would leave it undecided.
let estimate = simulate(meets(analysis=analysis, target=target),
                         seed=2026, runs=10000)
